  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 230 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms develop across \nsouthern portions of the area this afternoon.\n\nEarly this morning, a stationary boundary is located over far \nsoutheastern Virginia into northeast North Carolina. Slightly drier \nair has started to filter into far northern portions of the forecast \narea with wind turning to the N/NE. Near and south of the boundary, \ncontinued muggy conditions with a few lingering showers closer to \nthe Albemarle Sound and across eastern Currituck County. Patchy \nground fog will be possible for any areas that received rain on \nFriday. \n\nFor this afternoon, high pressure over the eastern Great Lakes will \ncontinue to nudge southward into the area, holding the weak boundary \nnear the VA/NC border. Surface heating south of US-460 will \ndestabilize the airmass (MLCAPE ~1500 J/kg), allowing for another \nround of scattered showers and thunderstorms to develop this \nafternoon. The highest coverage for any storms today will generally \nbe south of the VA/NC border across interior northeast North \nCarolina. Pulse-type storms will be capable of producing locally \nheavy rainfall and potentially a stronger wind gust. Areas north of \nUS-460 will see mainly dry conditions with partly sunny skies. High \ntemperatures today top out in the mid to upper 80s (cooler along the \nimmediate coast due to an onshore wind). Any convection tapers off \nrapidly after sunset as diurnal heating is lost. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions prevail during the early to middle \nportion of next week. \n\nHigh pressure centers offshore on Sunday, forcing the remnants of the \nboundary south of the local area. Outside of an isolated shower or \nstorm, mostly dry weather and partly sunny skies are expected across \nthe area. Highs on Sunday warm into the upper 80s to around 90.\n\nA strong 500mb upper level ridge expands eastward over the Ohio \nValley and into the Mid-Atlantic early next week. Warm advection and \ndeep mixing will allow for temperatures to rise into the low to mid \n90s on Monday. The ridge peaks in strength Tuesday into Wednesday \nbefore flattening later in the weak. Tuesday through Thursday will \nsee high temperatures likely in the mid to even upper 90s for many \ninland locations. Max heat indices through the period should \ngenerally average 100-104 due to deep mixing keeping dewpoints in \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s. However, if dewpoints overperform, a \nHeat Advisory may be needed across portions of southeast Virginia \nand northeast North Carolina. \n\nConvective chances remained capped near climatological minimums (15-\n20% or less) through Wednesday due to strong mid level capping. A \nbroad upper trough and associated cold front approach from the Great \nLakes Thursday into Friday, bringing the next meaningful opportunity \nfor showers and thunderstorms along with more seasonable \ntemperatures. AI models hint at the possibility for severe weather \non Thursday as the front approaches the area, this will be something \nto watch as we head into early next week.",
